WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.220
Here's a different hem treatment.
That's great

2
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.340
when knitting highly textured stitch

3
00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:12.480
patterns like G carriage
or tuck stitch patterns.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.180
We'll be reversing the standard hung hem.

5
00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:20.480
So put on your thinking
cap and let's get started.

6
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.580
One thing to consider is the fact
that most G carriage and tuck stitch

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.660
patterns work up wider
than stockinette stitch.

8
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:37.100
If you like your hems to pull
in a bit like ribbing, great.

9
00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:43.340
If not, you may need to add a few stitches
to your Caston to make the hem wider.

10
00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:48.480
Then decrease evenly before
starting your stitch pattern.

11
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:55.060
First, establish the public
and the private side of your knitting.

12
00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:57.280
In other words, which side of the garment

13
00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:00.900
is going to be facing you
when you're knitting?

14
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.620
For this sample, I've set up my G carriage

15
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:09.180
pattern so that the wrong or the private
side of the garment is facing me,

16
00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.570
just like I normally do if I
was knitting stockinette.

17
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.980
But I do need to keep this in mind when

18
00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:20.100
hanging the reverse Stockinette
or the mock-garter hem.

19
00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:23.140
I'm going to e-wrap Caston.

20
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:31.060
If you prefer Cast on with waste yarn and
Ravel cord, but I find e-Wrap to be faster.

21
00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.340
Even though this is going
to be a G-carriage sweater.

22
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:38.380
I'm using my main carriage for the hem.

23
00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:41.700
If you're familiar with knitting regular

24
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:47.490
stockinette stitch hung hems,
this part is going to be the same.

25
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.240
We will knit half of the hem at a
slightly tighter than garment tension.

26
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.100
We're going to call the private side

27
00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:01.020
the facing. So this will be
the facing, that first half.

28
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.900
In this case, I knit twelve rows.

29
00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:10.170
Now knit a turning row at a larger
tension, a looser tension.

30
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.700
Next, knit the other half of the hem,
which will be the public side of the hem.

31
00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.580
At garment tension.

32
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.890
Scrap your work off or take
it off on a Garter bar.

33
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.060
Because I've established that the public

34
00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.820
side of my garment
is going to be away from me.

35
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:36.960
As I'm knitting, I'll turn and rehang
the stitches from the Garter bar.

36
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.700
Now the knit side of the hem
will be facing me.

37
00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.060
Think of it this way.

38
00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:51.080
I want to encase the stockinette side of this piece

39
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:55.160
and let the Purl side show.

40
00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.460
Once the hem is back on the machine,

41
00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:04.100
pick up the eWrap Caston
stitches back on those needles.

42
00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:09.020
This time the purl side
of the facing is towards you.

43
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:14.920
Knit one row at a loose tension
across all the stitches.

44
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.780
Reset your carriage to garment tension,

45
00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:25.420
or start up your Garter carriage
and knit your garment piece.

46
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:31.540
This tuck stitch stitch pattern
will be knit with the Purl side-

47
00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:37.340
the public side will be facing
me as I knit the garment.

48
00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.500
In this case,

49
00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:45.580
we'll do one thing a little bit
differently to knit a faux Garter hem

50
00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:49.500
for a right side facing
textured stitch pattern.

51
00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.780
Knit the hem the same way.

52
00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:59.000
E-wrap cast on. Knit the facing of your
band at a slightly tighter than garment

53
00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:03.380
tension. Knit a loose turning row.

54
00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:09.100
Knit the public side of the hem
and remove on waist yarn.

55
00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:13.280
This time a Garter bar is
not going to help you.

56
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.540
You're going to hang the e-Wrap Caston

57
00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:23.740
with the knit side facing you or
the stockinette side facing you.

58
00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:30.260
Then rehang the hem stitches
from the waste yarn to form the hem.

59
00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:36.940
So it's this section you'll be doing
backwards from the first method.

60
00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:42.140
Knit one row plain and begin
knitting your garment.

61
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:48.460
I hope you'll think about mixing things up
when you knit your next garment with hems.

62
00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.140
Thank you for watching the Knit.

63
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.420
It Now Learning Library has hundreds

64
00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:58.660
of techniques on a variety
of subjects at all skill levels.

65
00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:04.340
Please consider a subscription
and let us help you be a better machine

66
00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:04.560
knitter.

